WEBVTT

NOTE Chapter: 1. The Coffee Shop Dilemma: What is Inflation?

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.810
In 1970, a cup of coffee cost twenty-five cents.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.100
Today, that exact same coffee costs four dollars.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.180
Did the coffee beans become sixteen times more delicious?

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.980
Of course not.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.890
The coffee didn't change; the value of the dollar did!

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:26.040
Inflation is not simply prices rising; it is the silent evaporation of your money's purchasing power over time.

NOTE Chapter: 2. The Twin Drivers: Demand-Pull vs. Cost-Push

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.790
Economists categorize inflation into two primary engines.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:40.450
First is Demand-Pull inflation: too much money chasing too few goods, like when stimulus cash arrives but factories cannot produce goods fast enough.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:51.240
Second is Cost-Push inflation: when production costs spike, such as an oil embargo or war that makes transportation and electricity expensive across the entire economy.

NOTE Chapter: 3. The Money Supply Cycle: Milton Friedman's Rule

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:58.700
As Nobel laureate Milton Friedman famously argued, inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:06.090
If an economy produces 100 apples, and there is 100 dollars in circulation, an apple costs 1 dollar.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:13.600
If the central bank prints another 100 dollars without growing more apples, an apple naturally rises to 2 dollars.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:20.660
The money supply cycle connects central bank interest rates, commercial bank lending, and the velocity of money.

NOTE Chapter: 4. The Central Bank's Sledgehammer: Raising Rates

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:27.020
To stop runaway inflation, central banks have one primary lever: hiking interest rates.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:34.570
When the Federal Reserve raises rates, borrowing costs for credit cards, cars, and home mortgages skyrocket.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:41.860
Consumers stop buying homes, companies freeze hiring, demand cools down, and prices stabilize.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:48.070
But the risk is immense: tighten too fast, and you trigger a painful economic recession.

NOTE Chapter: 5. Protecting Your Wealth: Assets That Beat Inflation

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.680
Inflation is an invisible tax on savers.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:57.690
Holding cash in a zero-interest savings account guarantees you lose real wealth every single year.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:09.780
To thrive, smart investors allocate capital into inflation-hedging assets: productive businesses with pricing power, real estate with rent growth, and Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.140
Understanding inflation turns you from an economic victim into an informed investor.
